<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morelos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Morelos is a small state of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, with Cuernavaca as its capital. Its warm valleys were settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> peoples and contain the hilltop city of Xochicalco, and in colonial times it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>-growing region. Created in 1869 and named after the independence leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a>, it was the heartland of Emiliano Zapata's revolution from 1911.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morelos.jpg" alt="Morelos" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morelos_in_Mexico_(zoom).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and 500 BCE: in burials at Tlatilco in the Valley of Mexico, on the reliefs of Chalcatzingo in <b>Morelos</b>, in the painted caves of Juxtlahuaca and Oxtotitlán and at Teopantecuanitlan in Guerrero, at San José Mogote in the Valley of Oaxaca, and on carved boulders at Pijijiapan in…</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldaderas and camp followers, from young girls to middle-aged women and often of mestiza or indigenous origin, came from Oaxaca, <b>Morelos</b>, Tehuantepec, Central Mexico, Sonora and elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-adelita">La Adelita</a></li><li><blockquote>Its fertile lands and chinampas produced maize and other crops, and it controlled routes from the Valley of Mexico to <b>Morelos</b> and Puebla.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalco">Chalco</a></li><li><blockquote>Xochicalco was built around 650 on a group of hills in western <b>Morelos</b>, as power in central Mexico fragmented after the decline of Teotihuacan.</blockquote><a href="/state/xochicalco">Xochicalco</a></li><li><blockquote>Xochimilco was ruled by its own tlatoque and controlled a domain stretching into the hills of <b>Morelos</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/xochimilco">Xochimilco</a></li></ul></section>
